    tldr: "IRMA compared ten twice-daily radiotherapy treatments to part of the breast with radiotherapy to the whole breast after surgery for low-risk breast cancer. At five years the cancer had returned in the treated breast in about 1 percent of patients in both groups. The difference was under 0.2 percentage points, but the trial's formal test that the shorter treatment is no worse was not met.",
    summary: "IRMA is a randomised, open-label phase 3 trial at 35 centres in patients aged 49 or over with low-risk invasive breast cancer treated by breast-conserving surgery. It compared external beam accelerated partial-breast irradiation, 38.5 Gy in ten fractions given twice daily, with whole-breast irradiation. The primary endpoint was ipsilateral breast tumour recurrence, with a non-inferiority margin for the hazard ratio of 1.5; the design assumed a five-year recurrence rate of 4 percent with whole-breast treatment.\n\nBetween 2007 and 2019, 3,309 patients were randomised. At a median follow-up of 97.5 months, the five-year cumulative incidence of recurrence in the treated breast was 1.0 percent (95% CI 0.6 to 1.6) with partial-breast irradiation and 0.8 percent (95% CI 0.5 to 1.4) with whole-breast irradiation. The hazard ratio was 1.18 (90% CI 0.63 to 2.20); because the upper limit crossed the margin, formal non-inferiority was not demonstrated, which is why this record is marked negative. The authors conclude that, with an absolute difference of less than 0.2 percent, external beam partial-breast irradiation is a reasonable clinical alternative for low-risk patients.\n\nThe 2026 abstract reports no late toxicity or cosmetic outcomes. RAPID, which used the same twice-daily schedule, found recurrence non-inferior but late toxicity and cosmesis worse than with whole-breast treatment, and NSABP B-39/RTOG 0413 did not establish equivalence; the partial-breast irradiation page sets the trials side by side.",
    result: "Five-year ipsilateral breast tumour recurrence 1.0 percent with twice-daily partial-breast irradiation against 0.8 percent with whole-breast irradiation; hazard ratio 1.18 (90% CI 0.63 to 2.20), upper limit above the non-inferiority margin of 1.5, so non-inferiority was not formally demonstrated.",
